To set up real-time Twitter data ingestion using Kafka on Ubuntu, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. **Install Java**: Kafka is built on the Java Platform, so you need to have Java installed.
2. **Install Apache Kafka**: This includes downloading Kafka and configuring it.
3. **Install Twitter4J**: This Java library is used to interact with the Twitter API.
4. **Set up Twitter Developer Account and API Keys**: You need to create a Twitter Developer account and obtain the necessary API keys and access tokens.
5. **Create a Kafka Producer to Produce Tweets**: You can write a Java program that uses Twitter4J to tweet and Kafka to produce these tweets to a Kafka topic.

### Step-by-Step Guide

#### 1. Install Java

Open a terminal and run:
```sh
sudo apt update
sudo apt install default-jdk
```

#### 2. Install Apache Kafka

First, download Kafka:
```sh
wget https://downloads.apache.org/kafka/3.3.0/kafka_2.13-3.3.0.tgz
```

Extract the archive:
```sh
tar -xzf kafka_2.13-3.3.0.tgz
```

Move the Kafka directory to `/opt`:
```sh
sudo mv kafka_2.13-3.3.0 /opt/kafka
```

#### 3. Install Twitter4J

Add Twitter4J Maven dependency to your project. If you're using a Maven project, add the following dependency to your `pom.xml`:
```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.twitter4j</groupId>
    <artifactId>twitter4j-stream</artifactId>
    <version>4.0.7</version>
</dependency>
```

Alternatively, you can download Twitter4J and add the JAR file to your classpath:
```sh
wget https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/twitter4j/twitter4j-stream/4.0.7/twitter4j-stream-4.0.7.jar
```

#### 4. Set Up Twitter Developer Account and API Keys

Create a Twitter Developer account and create an app to get your API keys (API key, API secret key, Access token, Access token secret).

#### 5. Create a Kafka Producer to Produce Tweets

Create a Java program to produce tweets from Twitter using Kafka. Here's a simple example:

```java
import kafka.javaapi.producer.Producer;
import kafka.producer.KafkaProducer;
import kafka.producer.ProducerRecord;
import twitter4j.Status;
import twitter4j.Twitter;
import twitter4j.TwitterException;
import twitter4j.auth.AccessToken;
import twitter4j.conf.ConfigurationBuilder;

public class TweetProducer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.setProperty("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "localhost:9092");
        String[] topic = {"tweets"};

        ConfigurationBuilder cb = new ConfigurationBuilder();
        cb.setDebugEnabled(true)
                .setOAuthConsumerKey("YOUR_CONSUMER_KEY")
                .setOAuthConsumerSecret("YOUR_CONSUMER_SECRET")
                .setOAuthAccessToken("YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN")
                .setOAuthAccessTokenSecret("YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET");

        Twitter twitter = new TwitterFactory(cb.build()).getInstance();

        TwitterStream twitterStream = new TwitterStreamFactory(cb.build()).getInstance();
        StatusMessageListener listener = (status) -> {
            Producer<ByteArrayOutputStream, String> producer = null;
            try {
                ByteArrayOutputStream byteArrayOutputStream = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
                producer = new KafkaProducer<>(byteArrayOutputStream.toByteArray(), new StringSerializer(), new StringSerializer());
                producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("tweets", status.getText()));
            } catch (TwitterException e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            } finally {
                if (producer != null) {
                    producer.close();
                }
            }
        };

        FilterQuery filterQuery = new FilterQuery();
        filterQuery.track(Arrays.asList("python", "java", "kafka")); // Example: Track certain keywords
        twitterStream.addListener(listener);
        twitterStream.filter(filterQuery);
    }
}
```

Make sure to replace `"YOUR_CONSUMER_KEY"`, `"YOUR_CONSUMER_SECRET"`, `"YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN"`, and `"YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN_SECRET"` with your actual Twitter API keys.

#### 6. Run the Java Program

Compile and run the Java program:
```sh
cd /opt/kafka
mvn compile
mvn exec:java -Dexec.mainClass="TweetProducer"
```

#### 7. Start Kafka Broker

Start the Kafka broker if it's not already running:
```sh
cd /opt/kafka/kafka_2.13-3.3.0
bin/kafka-server-start.sh config/server.properties
```

#### 8. Verify the Setup

You can use Kafka consumer to read and verify the data coming in:
```sh
cd /opt/kafka/kafka_2.13-3.3.0
bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic tweets --from-beginning
```

This will print out the tweets as they are produced.

That's it! You've now set up a real-time Twitter data ingestion pipeline using Kafka on Ubuntu.

In Vue.js, you can use the `v-if` directive to conditionally render elements based on certain conditions. However, `v-if` is not the best choice when you want to transition between visible and invisible states frequently, as it can cause unnecessary re-renders. Instead, you can use the `v-show` directive or CSS transitions to smoothly transition between the visible and invisible states. 

Here is an example of how you can use the `v-show` directive to toggle the visibility of an element:

```html
<div id="app">
  <div v-show="isVisible">
    This element is visible when isVisible is true.
  </div>
  <button v-on:click="toggleVisibility">Toggle Visibility</button>
</div>
```

```javascript
new Vue({
  el: '#app',
  data: {
    isVisible: true,
  },
  methods: {
    toggleVisibility: function() {
      this.isVisible = !this.isVisible;
    }
  }
});
```

In this example, the `div` element will be shown or hidden depending on the value of the `isVisible` data property. The `toggleVisibility` method changes the value of `isVisible` when the button is clicked.

To handle the transition between the visible and invisible states smoothly, you can add CSS transitions to the `div` element:

```css
#app div {
  transition: all 0.3s ease-out;
  opacity: 0;
}

#app div:not([v-show="false"]) {
  opacity: 1;
}
```

In this CSS, the `div` element starts with an opacity of 0 and is smoothly transitioned to an opacity of 1 when `v-show` is set to true.

Alternatively, if you want to use the `v-if` directive, you can use `keep-alive` to retain the state of the component when it is not visible, and then rehydrate it when it becomes visible again:

```html
<div id="app">
  <keep-alive>
    <div v-if="isVisible">
      This element is visible when isVisible is true.
    </div>
  </keep-alive>
  <button v-on:click="toggleVisibility">Toggle Visibility</button>
</div>
```

In this example, the `keep-alive` component retains the state of the `div` element when it is not visible, and rehydrates it when it becomes visible again.

```javascript
new Vue({
  el: '#app',
  data: {
    isVisible: true,
  },
  methods: {
    toggleVisibility: function() {
      this.isVisible = !this.isVisible;
    }
  }
});
```

In this case, the `div` element is destroyed and recreated when `isVisible` is changed.

